EXERCISED PATH: model/layers.py:169-182, model/frechet_poincare.py:73-98, train_rqvae.py:435-459 | I followed every leaf of the autograd graph and every dtype/clamp in the curved arithmetic, and checked that each switch changes the value of an executed expression. Live gradient carriers: reconstruction loss -> decoder and, through the straight-through term x_q = x + (x_q-x).detach() (layers.py:182), to the encoder; the weighted curved commitment (layers.py:176-177) -> point = clamp_ball(exp_0(latent)) -> encoder; the tangent MSE F.mse_loss(x, x_q.detach()) (layers.py:178) -> encoder. The codebook is deliberately off the gradient path: embed.weight.requires_grad_(False) at layers.py:84 keeps it out of AdamW, and the only writer is the detached estimator at model.py:78-80, which is the proposal's design, so no term is computed and then unused. Numerics: exp_0/clamp_ball/exp_at keep every code strictly inside the ball (BALL_EPS=1e-4); atanh arguments are clamped to 1-1e-7 in float64 (frechet_poincare.py:77,84,97-98); weight_sum is clamped at 1e-6 and empty or non-finite slots are frozen (frechet_poincare.py:191-199); mobius denominators are clamped at 1e-12, so no division by zero, no inf, no NaN. The only large factor (d atanh near the cap) is bounded and further contained by clip_grad_norm_=1.0 (train_rqvae.py:445-446). Switching use_behaviour off changes the tangent sum and the commitment (ones instead of w); switching use_curvature off changes distances, commitment, tangent and step to the Euclidean ones; so the coupled pathway changes both the resulting SID assignment and the encoder gradient in both directions.
FINDING SEVERITY: minor
FINDING LOCATION: model/layers.py:199-200
FINDING DETAIL: VQLayer.init_codebook passes the raw Euclidean latent x into the curved assignment (self.frechet.distances(x, self.get_code_embs())) while every training assignment first maps it into the ball (point = clamp_ball(exp_0(latent)), layers.py:153). The encoder latent has norm of order 1-3, i.e. frequently outside the unit ball, so many |mobius_add(-x, c)| exceed 1 and the distance saturates at the clamp 2*atanh(1-1e-7) ~ 16.1 for every slot, and argmin then returns slot 0 for every item. This is minor because that assignment only produces the returned residual (layers.py:201), and that return value is discarded: RQVAE.init_codebook (model.py:82-85) ignores it and RQLayer.init_codebook's residual (layers.py:355-357) is never used. The codebooks actually written come from KMeans on x (level 1) and on a constant-shifted copy of x (levels 2-3), whose partition and warm start are unchanged, and the first training step re-assigns with the proper ball points.
FINDING REPRODUCTION: encode one batch with the real Xavier-initialised encoder, take x with |x|_2 > 1 (the 128->32 head easily reaches ~2), and compute self.frechet.distances(x, exp_0(KMeans centres)); every row comes out identical at ~16.1 and torch.argmin(..., dim=-1) is identically 0, whereas the training path uses clamp_ball(exp_0(x)) and gives a spread assignment.
FINDING CORRECTION: in VQLayer.init_codebook use the same point as training: point = clamp_ball(exp_0(x)), assign with self.frechet.distances(point, codebook), and return x - log_0(self.embed_code(embed_ind)).
